    {
      "id": "intro_t",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 14,
      "y": 90,
      "width": 1772,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "f0",
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1397871145,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 103694313,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793750000000,
      "created": 1793750000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What this chart is about\n- Northbridge Cycles (Pune, Maharashtra) ordered 18,000 battery packs from Voltway Cells (Gujarat) at Rs.1,276.80 each (order PO/2026/01120). Both are Indian residents;\n  GST is 18%.\n- The contract says: the price on the delivery date applies; the cash-discount tiers (0.25% within 15 days, 0.125% within 30) count from the delivery date and reduce the\n  GST too, but a discount agreed after the supply cannot reduce GST (CGST Act s.15(3)(b)); defective goods must be claimed from Voltway within 2 days; goods broken in\n  transit are Northbridge's risk, claimed from its insurer.\n- Voltway's price became Rs.1,332.80 on 1st October. The packs arrived on 2nd October; 300 were broken in transit. Invoice 4471 bills 18,000 at Rs.1,332.80. It is due on\n  16th November; Northbridge would normally pay it in the 20th November run.\n- On 7th October Voltway emailed better terms (0.5% if paid within 15 days of delivery, 0.25% within 30) and called Emma the next day. Voltway never learns about\n  Northbridge's holds or internal steps. Paying early is not free: the assistant asks Treasury what the money would earn if kept (6.5% a year), the discount loses, and\n  Vikram declines it.\n- This invoice takes Voltway's purchases this year past Rs.50 lakh, so TDS starts (Income-tax Act 2025, s.393(1), formerly 194Q). The packs came with Saurashtra Roadways,\n  whose freight (Rs.1,20,000) is under reverse charge: Northbridge itself owes IGST at 5%, Rs.6,000.\n- After the invoice: GST credit is claimed only after GSTR-2B confirms it; the GST on the 300 broken packs is blocked credit; TDS is deposited by the 7th, returned\n  quarterly and certified. Meera Kulkarni, the tax manager, approves every tax figure.\n- Frame 1 (the top band): today, by hand, with red notes where it goes wrong. Frame 2 (the band under it, same phases): with the assistant in place from the start. The\n  agent drafts every entry, and a person approves or amends each one within 24 hours (frame 3). Every morning it checks the bank's emails against the bank statement (frame\n  4). The tables (money, jobs, guardrails, tests) are in CAPSTONE_design.md.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "intro",
      "originalText": "What this chart is about\n- Northbridge Cycles (Pune, Maharashtra) ordered 18,000 battery packs from Voltway Cells (Gujarat) at Rs.1,276.80 each (order PO/2026/01120). Both are Indian residents;\n  GST is 18%.\n- The contract says: the price on the delivery date applies; the cash-discount tiers (0.25% within 15 days, 0.125% within 30) count from the delivery date and reduce the\n  GST too, but a discount agreed after the supply cannot reduce GST (CGST Act s.15(3)(b)); defective goods must be claimed from Voltway within 2 days; goods broken in\n  transit are Northbridge's risk, claimed from its insurer.\n- Voltway's price became Rs.1,332.80 on 1st October. The packs arrived on 2nd October; 300 were broken in transit. Invoice 4471 bills 18,000 at Rs.1,332.80. It is due on\n  16th November; Northbridge would normally pay it in the 20th November run.\n- On 7th October Voltway emailed better terms (0.5% if paid within 15 days of delivery, 0.25% within 30) and called Emma the next day. Voltway never learns about\n  Northbridge's holds or internal steps. Paying early is not free: the assistant asks Treasury what the money would earn if kept (6.5% a year), the discount loses, and\n  Vikram declines it.\n- This invoice takes Voltway's purchases this year past Rs.50 lakh, so TDS starts (Income-tax Act 2025, s.393(1), formerly 194Q). The packs came with Saurashtra Roadways,\n  whose freight (Rs.1,20,000) is under reverse charge: Northbridge itself owes IGST at 5%, Rs.6,000.\n- After the invoice: GST credit is claimed only after GSTR-2B confirms it; the GST on the 300 broken packs is blocked credit; TDS is deposited by the 7th, returned\n  quarterly and certified. Meera Kulkarni, the tax manager, approves every tax figure.\n- Frame 1 (the top band): today, by hand, with red notes where it goes wrong. Frame 2 (the band under it, same phases): with the assistant in place from the start. The\n  agent drafts every entry, and a person approves or amends each one within 24 hours (frame 3). Every morning it checks the bank's emails against the bank statement (frame\n  4). The tables (money, jobs, guardrails, tests) are in CAPSTONE_design.md.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
